"""Chapters 1 and 2."""
from docx.enum.text import WD_ALIGN_PARAGRAPH as AL

C, L = AL.CENTER, AL.LEFT


def ch1(R):
    R.H1("Chapter 1: Introduction")
    R.H2("1.1 Background")
    R.P("Network forensics is the capture, recording and analysis of network events in order to find the source of attacks and other incidents [1]. Its raw material is traffic recorded at some point on a network. The recording is made by a capture program such as tcpdump, windump or tshark, which can be used from the command line, or by Wireshark, which adds a graphical view [1]. The program stores frames, that is, Ethernet frames that carry IP packets, which in turn carry TCP or UDP data, in a capture file usually called a pcap file. Once saved, the file shows what really crossed the wire, who talked to whom and what was sent, even when logs are missing.")
    R.P("A pile of frames only becomes an account of an incident when the frames are put in order. For this reason every captured frame is stored with a time. Messier compares a badly ordered timeline with a person who drives to work, takes a shower and only then gets out of bed: every event is real, but the story makes no sense [1]. Computers are fast and many things happen inside a single second, so the finer the grain of the time, the more precisely events can be ordered.")
    R.FIG("1", "fig_1_1_frame_vs_record.png", "Where the time comes from: a frame on the wire has no arrival time; the capture record does")
    R.P("A common misconception, and the starting point of this study, is that the time is part of the packet. It is not. An Ethernet frame carries addresses, a type, a payload and a check sequence, and the IP header carries addresses, a time-to-live, a protocol and an identification field. None of these fields says when the frame reached the capture point. The time is added by the capture program, which reads the clock of the capture host at the moment it sees the frame and writes the value into a small record header beside the frame bytes, as Fig. 1.1 shows. The consequence is that a packet time is only as good as four things: the clock that produced it, the precision with which it is stored, the way it is displayed, and the care taken when it is compared with times from other systems.")
    R.H2("1.2 Industry/Domain Context")
    R.P("Security operations centres and incident-response teams rarely work from one source. They compare packet captures with firewall logs, server logs, endpoint security alerts and the alerts of network monitoring systems, and increasingly they feed all of these into a Security Information and Event Management (SIEM) system that correlates them [1]. The shared key in all of this is time. When one device logs in local time, another in UTC and a third has a clock that is wrong, the combined timeline can place effects before their causes. Three situations from the seminar on which this report is based show why packet capture times matter in practice (Table 1.1).")
    R.H3("1.2.1 The Target data breach, 2013")
    R.P("Target, one of the largest retailers in the United States, is the real-world case of this study. According to the staff report of the U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation, the attackers reportedly began with credentials stolen from Fazio Mechanical Services, a small Pennsylvania HVAC vendor that had remote access to Target for billing and project work [2]. From that foothold, weak separation inside the network reportedly allowed them to reach point-of-sale terminals, where tailored RAM-scraping malware read card data from memory before it was encrypted. The data was gathered on internal servers and sent in plain text by FTP to outside servers, at least one of them in Russia. About 40 million card records were exposed, and Target later said that personal data on up to 70 million people had also been stolen [2], [3]. Target’s own detection software reportedly raised alerts that were not acted on, and the company learned of the breach from the U.S. Department of Justice [2], [3]. The Senate staff analysed the events with the Lockheed Martin intrusion kill chain [2], [16]. The staff report itself says that it relied on media reports and expert analyses, so several details are reported rather than proven; this report keeps that caution by using the word “reportedly” where the source does.")
    R.H3("1.2.2 Memcached amplification against GitHub, 2018")
    R.P("On 28 February 2018 GitHub was hit by a distributed denial-of-service attack that used memcached servers exposed to the internet to amplify small requests into very large responses. The peak was reported as 1.35 terabits per second; GitHub’s monitoring detected the anomaly, traffic was moved through a scrubbing provider, and service was restored within minutes [17]. For this study the event is an example of a case where the question that matters is about time: how quickly was the anomaly seen, and how long did it last. The source was consulted only through a summary of the incident report, so the figures should be checked against the original post before they are quoted elsewhere.")
    R.H3("1.2.3 SYN floods and identical timestamps")
    R.P("Messier’s discussion of SYN floods supplies a small, concrete example of timing as evidence. In a tcpdump capture of a flood the timestamps look identical on every frame because the SYN messages were sent about a microsecond apart; a SYN and its SYN/ACK in a normal listing differ by tens of microseconds [1]. The extraordinary number of frames per second is itself the signature of the attack.")
    R.TABLE("1", "Scenarios used in this case study and the time-related lesson of each",
            ["Scenario", "What happened (source)", "Lesson about packet times"],
            [["Target breach, Nov.–Dec. 2013", "Vendor credentials, POS malware, FTP exfiltration over about two weeks; alerts not acted on; Justice Department notified Target on 12 Dec. [2], [3]",
              "Attack and detection unfold over weeks; the daily window of outbound transfers is a time-based signature"],
             ["GitHub, 28 Feb. 2018", "Memcached amplification DDoS, peak 1.35 Tbps, resolved within minutes [17]",
              "Frame counts per second reveal a flood; start and end times measure the impact"],
             ["SYN flood (Messier)", "Many SYNs, SYN/ACKs without final ACKs; stamps identical a microsecond apart [1]",
              "Microsecond resolution matters; whole-second stamps would hide the pattern"],
             ["Mixed-source incident (hypothetical)", "Packets, firewall, server, endpoint and SIEM records with different zones and clocks",
              "Records must be converted to one reference and corrected for measured offsets before merging"]],
            [1.35, 2.5, 1.92], size=9.5)
    R.H2("1.3 Problem Statement")
    R.P("Packet capture times are used every day as evidence, yet their origin and limits are poorly understood. Investigators often assume that the time is a property of the packet, read relative times as if they were clock times, mix local time with UTC, and trust a capture host’s clock without checking it. Each of these habits can turn a correct capture into a wrong timeline. The problem addressed by this case study is therefore twofold. First, where does a packet capture time come from, how is it stored in a capture file and how is it read in tcpdump and Wireshark? Second, what has to be done so that these times can be correlated safely with other evidence, using the Target breach as the example of why the correlation matters?")
    R.H2("1.4 Objectives")
    R.P("The objectives of the study are:", after=3)
    R.bullets([
        "to explain, and to demonstrate on a real capture file, that the arrival time is added by the capture program and stored in the pcap record header as seconds since the epoch plus a microsecond (or nanosecond) fraction;",
        "to read the same capture with tcpdump, Wireshark’s command-line tool tshark and an independent parser, and to compare relative, absolute and epoch time displays;",
        "to quantify the effects of time zones, clock drift and unequal precision on the correlation of evidence, and to show a repeatable procedure that corrects them;",
        "to apply these ideas to the 2013 Target breach by building its attack-and-response timeline from the Senate staff report and by showing, with clearly labelled synthetic data, how packet times would reveal a daily exfiltration window; and",
        "to derive practical recommendations for capturing, storing and reporting packet times."])
    R.H2("1.5 Scope and Limitations")
    R.P("The study covers the origin, storage, display and correlation of packet capture times, the roles of UTC and the Network Time Protocol (NTP), clock drift, and timeline reconstruction using packet captures, logs and alerts. It does not cover hardware timestamping in specialised network cards, the Precision Time Protocol, or the full analysis of malware in the Target case.")
    R.P("There are five limits that the reader should keep in mind. First, no real packet capture of the Target breach is available, so none is claimed; the Target chapter uses publicly reported dates, and the packet-level analysis for it is a labelled illustration. Second, the five-frame capture was constructed, with real Ethernet, IP and TCP headers but timestamps chosen to match the sample values of the seminar; its payloads are placeholder bytes. Third, the live capture was taken on the loopback interface of a Linux container, so its microsecond gaps show how timestamps behave but not the delay of a real network. Fourth, the exfiltration data set and the multi-source incident of Section 5.1.4 are synthetic or hypothetical and are included to demonstrate a method; their outcomes follow from how they were built. Fifth, Target facts come from a Senate staff report that was itself compiled from media reports, and the chapter references to Messier’s book follow the seminar slides; the page numbers given there were not re-checked against the book.")
    R.H2("1.6 Organization of the Report")
    R.P("Chapter 2 reviews the literature and tool documentation on packet times and timeline analysis and identifies the gap. Chapter 3 describes the approach, the data sets, the architecture of capture and normalisation, and the tools. Chapter 4 walks through the execution, the core algorithms and the problems met on the way. Chapter 5 presents the findings, discusses them and draws out the practical implications. Chapter 6 concludes and proposes future work. The references and appendices with scripts, outputs and reproduction steps follow. Table 1.2 shows how the study relates to the units of the course.")
    R.TABLE("1", "Mapping of the case study to the 21CSE535T course units",
            ["Course unit", "Topics used in this case study", "CO"],
            [["Unit 1: Introduction to Network Forensics", "Evidence value of captures and logs; the Target case as an incident narrative", "CO-1"],
             ["Unit 2: Packet Capture and Analysis", "tcpdump, tshark and Wireshark; pcap and pcapng files; time display formats; SPAN ports", "CO-2"],
             ["Unit 3: Location Awareness", "Capture location: a host sees only its own traffic on a switch", "CO-3"],
             ["Unit 4: Intrusion Detection System", "Alert times against traffic times; alerts lag the activity they report", "CO-4"],
             ["Unit 5: Correlating Attacks", "Timelines, UTC, NTP, clock drift, SIEM, kill chain; the Target timeline", "CO-5"]],
            [2.0, 3.2, 0.57], size=9.5)


def ch2(R):
    R.H1("Chapter 2: Literature Survey")
    R.H2("2.1 Introduction")
    R.P("The work relevant to this case falls into four groups. The first describes how packet capture and its files work: the textbook and the documentation of the tools and formats. The second sets the standards for keeping clocks correct and for handling time in investigations. The third deals with timeline analysis, mainly from the digital forensics of hosts and disks, which supplies methods that also apply to network evidence. The fourth supplies the framework used to analyse the Target breach. Fig. 2.1 summarises, as a chain, the places where the accuracy of a packet time can be lost; the sources reviewed below are discussed against these stages.")
    R.FIG("2", "fig_2_1_error_chain.png", "Five places where the accuracy of a packet time can be lost")
    R.H2("2.2 Review of Existing Approaches")
    R.H3("2.2.1 Network Forensics (Messier, 2017)")
    R.P("Messier’s book is the primary reference of this study [1]. Chapter 4, Packet Capture and Analysis, explains why capture should be done with free command-line tools (tcpdump, windump, tshark) that share the same options and need no graphical interface, because capturing needs administrative privileges and running a large graphical program with them exposes the system, and why analysis is then done in Wireshark. It also explains the visibility problem: a switch sends a host only the frames meant for it, so a SPAN port, a hub or ARP poisoning is needed to see more. Chapter 10, Correlating Attacks, develops timelines, the use of UTC and NTP, the Plaso tools, PacketTotal, Wireshark’s statistics and SIEM systems, and it warns that one system ten minutes off can skew an entire investigation. Chapter 5 supplies real tcpdump timestamps in its SYN-flood listing. The strength of the book is its practical narrative; its limit, for this study, is that it does not explain how the time is stored in the file or measure the effects it describes.")
    R.H3("2.2.2 NIST guidance on forensics and log management (2006)")
    R.P("NIST SP 800-86 describes how forensic techniques fit into incident response and treats reliable information about when events happened as a prerequisite for combining data from several sources [9]. NIST SP 800-92 on log management recommends that log-generating systems keep their clocks synchronised, for example with NTP, so that logs from different systems can be compared [10]. Both documents state the policy clearly but are written at the level of organisations and procedures; they do not show what a timestamp looks like in a capture or how large an unsynchronised error can become.")
    R.H3("2.2.3 The Network Time Protocol (RFC 5905, 2010)")
    R.P("NTP version 4 is specified in RFC 5905 [8]. Clients obtain time from servers arranged in strata, with stratum 0 being the reference clocks such as GPS receivers or atomic clocks, stratum 1 servers connected to them, and higher strata taking time from the level above. The protocol measures the offset and delay between client and server and filters its samples to discipline the local clock. NTP documentation generally reports that clocks can be held to within tens of milliseconds over the public internet and to better than a millisecond on a local network under good conditions. NTP therefore fixes the clock itself, which a change of time zone cannot do. Its limit for forensics is that it works only if it was actually configured and running on the capture host, and nothing in a capture file records whether it was.")
    R.H3("2.2.4 Capture-file formats and tool documentation")
    R.P("The libpcap savefile format is documented in the pcap-savefile manual page [4]. A file starts with a 24-byte global header whose magic number, 0xa1b2c3d4 or 0xa1b23c4d, tells a reader whether the fraction field of each record is in microseconds or nanoseconds. Each frame is preceded by a 16-byte record header with four 32-bit fields: whole seconds since the epoch, the fraction, the captured length and the original length. The newer pcapng format is block based; an Enhanced Packet Block stores a 64-bit timestamp, and the unit is declared in the interface description with the if_tsresol option [7]. The tcpdump manual page documents the options that control how the time is printed, namely -t, -tt, -ttt and -tttt [5], and the Wireshark User’s Guide documents the time display formats and time references [6]. These documents are accurate and authoritative but are reference material, not teaching material, and they do not discuss how a time display choice affects an investigation.")
    R.H3("2.2.5 The TCP timestamp option (RFC 7323, 2014)")
    R.P("RFC 7323 defines the TCP timestamp option, in which the sender places a clock value (TSval) and echoes the peer’s value (TSecr) in the TCP header, for round-trip time measurement and protection against wrapped sequence numbers [11]. The option is easily confused with the capture time because both are “timestamps in a packet”, but they are different things: the option is chosen by the sender, has an arbitrary unit and origin, and is not the time at which the capture point saw the frame. The constructed capture of this study includes the option in its frames, which allows the difference to be shown directly.")
    R.H3("2.2.6 Time frames and clock inconsistencies (Stevens, 2004; Weil, 2002)")
    R.P("Stevens addresses the problem of unifying the time frames of several computers whose clocks differ, so that their timestamps can be placed on one timeline [12]. Weil’s dynamic time and date stamp analysis starts from an observed mismatch between an intrusion detection system’s time and the victim host’s clock, and compares times found inside files with file-system times to detect clock errors instead of trusting a single reading of the system clock [13]. Both works concern host and file-system evidence but state the principle applied in this study to network evidence: a clock is a measurement that must be checked, and the offset must be estimated and recorded before events from different sources are merged.")
    R.H3("2.2.7 Timeline tools and automated reconstruction (2009, 2012)")
    R.P("Olsson and Boldt built CyberForensic TimeLab, a prototype that indexes evidence by its time variables and plots it on a single timeline, and they reported in a preliminary user test that it helped examiners find time-coherent evidence faster and with fewer errors than a commercial tool [14]. Hargreaves and Patterson go further by automatically deriving higher-level events, such as a USB device being connected, from low-level timestamped events, while keeping each inferred event linked to the raw data behind it [15]. Messier describes the tools of the same family for network work: Plaso for log data, PacketTotal for timelines of the sessions in a capture, and SIEM systems for combining event and flow data [1]. These tools help only once the times are comparable; they take the correctness of the clock for granted.")
    R.H3("2.2.8 Kill-chain analysis and the Senate report on Target (2011, 2014)")
    R.P("The intrusion kill chain of Hutchins, Cloppert and Amin divides an intrusion into reconnaissance, weaponization, delivery, exploitation, installation, command and control, and actions on objectives [16]. The Senate staff used it to analyse the Target breach and concluded that Target missed a number of opportunities to stop the attackers along that chain [2]. The report also gives the attack and response dates on which Fig. 5.5 of this study is based. It does not claim any packet-level times, and it states that it was compiled from media reports and expert analyses.")
    R.H2("2.3 Comparative Analysis and Gap Identification")
    R.P("Table 2.1 compares the sources on what each contributes to this case and what it leaves open.")
    R.TABLE("2", "Comparison of the reviewed sources",
            ["Source", "Contribution", "Limitation for this case"],
            [["Messier [1]", "Practical capture and correlation workflow; UTC and NTP advice", "Does not show how time is stored or measure its errors"],
             ["NIST SP 800-86, 800-92 [9], [10]", "Policy: reliable time and synchronised log clocks", "Organisation-level; no packet-level demonstration"],
             ["RFC 5905 (NTP) [8]", "Defines strata and clock discipline", "Does not record in a capture whether it was used"],
             ["pcap-savefile, pcapng, tool manuals [4]–[7]", "Exact file layout, magic numbers, display options", "Reference text; no investigative guidance"],
             ["RFC 7323 [11]", "Defines the TCP timestamp option", "Not the capture time; source of confusion"],
             ["Stevens; Weil [12], [13]", "Unifying clocks; detecting clock errors", "Host and file-system evidence, not packets"],
             ["Olsson and Boldt; Hargreaves and Patterson [14], [15]", "Timeline visualisation and automatic event reconstruction", "Assume that input times are already correct"],
             ["Kill chain; Senate report [2], [16]", "Framework and dated account of the Target breach", "No packet-level times; media-sourced"]],
            [1.9, 2.0, 1.87], size=9.5)
    R.P("None of these sources puts the whole chain of Fig. 2.1 in one place. The textbook and the standards say that time matters and that clocks must be synchronised; the format documents say how the value is stored; the timeline literature says how to merge times but largely assumes that they are right. What is missing, at the level of a student case study, is a small, reproducible demonstration that follows one packet time from the capture program to the final merged timeline, measures the errors that can enter on the way, and ties the result to a real incident. This study fills that gap by building and decoding real capture files, by modelling drift, zone and precision effects with scripts that anyone can rerun, and by applying the results to the Target breach with the fact and the illustration kept apart.")
